import{_ as t,o as a,c as n,ak as i}from"./chunks/framework.B8jO0odJ.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Parallel game implementation plan","description":"Approved scope supersession — October 8, 2026: The user selected essential fundamentals for ages 12–17, especially underserved/remote learners with limited technology access, ","frontmatter":{"title":"Parallel game implementation plan","description":"Approved scope supersession — October 8, 2026: The user selected essential fundamentals for ages 12–17, especially underserved/remote learners with limited technology access, ","source":"docs/planning/implementation-plan.md","category":"Delivery & team","sourceUpdated":null,"outline":[2,3]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"content/docs/planning/implementation-plan.md","filePath":"content/docs/planning/implementation-plan.md"}'),r={name:"content/docs/planning/implementation-plan.md"};function o(s,e,d,c,l,p){return a(),n("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<div><h1 id="parallel-game-implementation-plan">Parallel game implementation plan<a class="header-anchor" href="#parallel-game-implementation-plan" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h1><p><strong>Approved scope supersession — October 8, 2026:</strong> The user selected essential fundamentals for ages 12–17, especially underserved/remote learners with limited technology access, through tappable Web3, Blockchain and AI worlds. Full A–Z coverage is withdrawn. Free spatial construction and mandatory walkable 3D/joystick navigation are deferred; use bounded order/link activities, guided three-local-replica comparison, a genuine tiny classifier validated before production, and a short fixed-grid agent. The nine lesson IDs W1–W3, B1–B3 and A1–A3 are a draft curriculum, not a confirmed count. Current baseline/GDD/curriculum take precedence over historical issue titles and old level IDs below. Phone specifications, SQLite/export, rubric and November 21 deadline remain. The old 520-hour estimate has not been recosted for this scope.</p><p>Prepared October 8, 2026. <strong>Pre-final defense: November 21, 2026 (44 calendar days away).</strong> This is an execution design, not proof of delivered functionality or a guarantee of school completion. Original CH issue dates remain publication history. The revised local October 8 forecast below supersedes the October 5 forecast after the user proposed November 3–6 beta testing at Tambunan National High School, Marinduque. Actual hours are unknown; the user authorized balanced planning estimates. Game implementation and actual device evidence remain unverified; rubric and phone specifications are retained.</p><h2 id="scope-and-authority">Scope and authority<a class="header-anchor" href="#scope-and-authority" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>Use the <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/project-baseline.html">baseline</a>, <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/requirements/product-requirements.html">PRD</a>, <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/game-design.html">GDD</a>, <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/requirements/technical-requirements.html">technical requirements</a>, <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/design/app-flow.html">flow</a>, <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/design/design-brief.html">design brief</a> and <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/design/database-schema.html">database schema</a>. User decisions override documents. Recommendations stay proposed until accepted or validated. Game implementation belongs in Chainy-Game; this repository owns management, specifications and existing board tooling.</p><p>CH-R01–12 retain traceability, with CH-R01/02 revised by user approval. Implement tappable Web3/Blockchain/AI lesson navigation, bounded order/link activities, guided three-replica comparison, a genuine tiny classifier after validation, a short fixed-grid agent, SQLite and teacher export. Full A–Z coverage is withdrawn; the nine-lesson curriculum is draft. Reduce art and content quantity before removing required learning concepts. No new gameplay backend is implied by the existing Cloudflare board-sync service.</p><p>Scope mentor verdict: <strong>validate first, then proceed with bounded mechanics</strong>. The strongest evidence is the unverified device/toolchain and capacity, the last zero-progress report, and mandatory pilot/manuscript work. Parallel agents can prepare isolated modules but cannot substitute for on-device integration, site approval, or human review. The next observable gate is a reproducible installed APK plus SQLite/relaunch/export evidence by the existing October 9–11 targets. If this fails, reforecast openly.</p><h2 id="phase-0-repository-organization-and-contract-preparation">Phase 0: repository organization and contract preparation<a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-0-repository-organization-and-contract-preparation" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>Historical proposals, classroom exercises and the stale issue draft are now under docs/archive. Their exact content is retained, including obsolete JSON and status statements. The <a href="/Chainy-Docs/attachments/docs/planning/repository-move-manifest.json">move manifest</a> records old/new paths and SHA-256 hashes. Active reference paths remain stable to preserve existing skill and board links. Root README/AGENTS, .agents, docs/backlog.json and tools/kanban-sync retain their operational roles. Ignored dist and .wrangler are generated/local state, not source documents to reorganize or delete.</p><p>The <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/documentation-index.html">documentation index</a> is the entry point. Each fact has one authority: baseline for accepted scope, PRD for acceptance, GDD for gameplay rules, technical design/contracts for implementation, SQL for table definitions, GitHub issues for execution status, backlog.json for publication inventory. Do not copy and independently edit the same rules in parallel.</p><p>Before implementation dispatch, read the applicable game repository AGENTS.md, inspect its current files and uncommitted changes, pin the base commit, and map proposed paths below to actual paths. The game source was not inspected during this management-document task. Do not treat these paths as already implemented.</p><h2 id="parallel-execution-model">Parallel execution model<a class="header-anchor" href="#parallel-execution-model" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>One coordinator dispatches bounded packets; a reviewer checks each handoff; Christian controls integration and shared Unity scenes. Human Lead/Support/Reviewer fields remain the existing named assignments, distinct from temporary AI agent roles. This plan combines parallel AI execution with named student-team ownership, consistent with the request to balance each member time commitment.</p><p>Use up to three concurrent worker agents plus one coordinator when four agent slots are available. Slots are a concurrency ceiling, not a staffing estimate. Run fewer lanes when review capacity or dependencies require it. Give each agent one packet, one base commit and an exclusive write allowlist. Read access can overlap. A specialist review does not automatically require another agent.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Lane</th><th>Human lead</th><th>Proposed exclusive output boundary in game repository</th><th>Shared inputs and review</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Foundation/persistence</td><td>Christian Jude Villaber</td><td>Runtime/Core, Runtime/Persistence, Runtime/Platform; bootstrap/integration scenes</td><td>Pinned toolchain, schema, save/export contract; Sherwin review, Miguel device execution</td></tr><tr><td>Order-link/blockchain</td><td>Christian Jude Villaber</td><td>Runtime/Gameplay/Activities, Runtime/Learning/Ledger; own test scenes</td><td>Stable mission and save interfaces; Sherwin review; educational checks by Sean</td></tr><tr><td>Classifier/agent/importer</td><td>Sherwin Limosnero</td><td>Runtime/Learning/Classifier, Runtime/Learning/Agent; separate staff importer directory</td><td>Versioned vectors/policy/export contract; Christian technical review</td></tr><tr><td>UI and assets</td><td>Miguel De Leon for UI; Luis Cabrido III for art</td><td>Miguel: Runtime/UI and UI prefabs. Luis: Art/Audio and model prefabs</td><td>Mock services until stable interfaces; no simultaneous prefab/scene edits</td></tr><tr><td>Research and manuscript</td><td>Sean Matthew Narvaez</td><td>Assigned manuscript sections and research drafts in management repo</td><td>Actual approved instruments and results; Miguel figures, Sherwin review</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These are workstreams, not five simultaneous worker slots or claims that Christian can deliver two lanes at once. Foundation first releases the bounded-activity lane; Sherwin must schedule site work and importer work alongside AI tasks. Sean&#39;s research work starts early and cannot wait for the finished game.</p><h2 id="contract-gate-before-concurrent-coding">Contract gate before concurrent coding<a class="header-anchor" href="#contract-gate-before-concurrent-coding" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>Christian coordinates a short reviewed contract change under CH-02/03/04. Define DTO names, field types, version policy, error codes and minimal tests in game source before consumers merge. Do not freeze an untested SQLite binding by documentation alone.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Contract</th><th>Required agreement</th><th>Consumer can proceed when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ActivityDefinition</td><td>ID/version, objective, component palette, validation rules, hints, quiz references</td><td>Two synthetic activities validate; unknown ID/version fails clearly</td></tr><tr><td>ActivitySnapshot</td><td>Stable lesson/activity IDs, supplied record order, predecessor choices, format/content version</td><td>Valid/invalid order-link fixtures restore identically; no free-spatial transforms required</td></tr><tr><td>ActivityResult</td><td>Attempt ID, session ID, activity/content version, outcome, duration, hints/retries</td><td>Retry reuses ID; a conflicting payload is rejected</td></tr><tr><td>SaveResult/Checkpoint</td><td>Atomic attempt/progress/checkpoint transaction; committed/retry/recovery outcomes</td><td>Consumer cannot show completion before commit</td></tr><tr><td>Classifier and agent</td><td>Numeric feature normalization, class/tie/missing-example results; capped policy trace</td><td>Deterministic fixtures pass without Unity scene dependencies</td></tr><tr><td>EvidencePackage</td><td>Versioned manifest, relationships, counts, digest, duplicate/conflict handling</td><td>Producer and importer validate the same synthetic golden package</td></tr><tr><td>Presentation</td><td>View events and result/error display; no direct DB writes from UI</td><td>Mock success, invalid input, save failure and export cancel screens work</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A proposed contract change must name affected consumers and migration/content-version impact. Coordinator pauses affected packets, updates contract and fixtures once, then redispatches. Unaffected lanes continue. Do not let agents independently rename IDs, introduce packages or alter shared schema.</p><h2 id="dependency-gates-and-dispatch-waves">Dependency gates and dispatch waves<a class="header-anchor" href="#dependency-gates-and-dispatch-waves" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p><a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/planning/agent-task-packets.html">Task packets</a> preserve the 26 existing task IDs, stable IDs, original issue-title references, original finish-to-start blockers, owners and targets; locally revised scope/acceptance supersede old issue text pending explicit publication. The <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/kanban-plan-draft.html">recovery schedule</a> remains the dated forecast. The table below adds safe dispatch guidance; it does not erase task blockers to manufacture parallelism.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Wave</th><th>Eligible work and exit evidence</th><th>Parallelism boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0: now</td><td>CH-01 rubric/capacity and CH-03 first APK; finish current documentation reconciliation</td><td>CH-01 is past its Oct 7 target with completion unverified; follow up immediately. No fabricated rescheduling</td></tr><tr><td>1: contracts and spikes</td><td>After CH-01, CH-02 bounded rules; CH-04 after CH-03; CH-24 site paperwork after CH-01</td><td>Device spike and research preparation can overlap with design drafts; blocked production tasks remain blocked</td></tr><tr><td>2: isolated components</td><td>CH-05 lesson shell/order-link, CH-08 classifier, CH-10 art, CH-26 UI after CH-02/03; CH-11 importer after CH-04; CH-22/23 writing after their blockers</td><td>Select at most three ready packets; use mocks/fixtures at stable boundaries; Christian cannot own both active foundation and bounded-activity delivery</td></tr><tr><td>3: first integrated slice</td><td>CH-06 after CH-04/05/26: complete the first agreed lesson slice on device including save and assessment; CH-12 protocol after CH-01/02/23/24</td><td>Art, AI prototype and manuscript work continue independently; no full-curriculum integration yet</td></tr><tr><td>4: remaining concepts</td><td>CH-07 after CH-06; CH-09 after CH-05/08; complete CH-10/11</td><td>Ledger and AI modules may run in parallel with separate scenes and fixed contracts</td></tr><tr><td>5: integration and freeze</td><td>CH-13 after CH-07/09/10/11/26, local target Oct 30 (previous Nov 6); CH-14 pilot-ready device/update checks Oct 31–Nov 2 (previous Nov 7)</td><td>One integration writer; other agents perform bounded reviews/tests and fixes in owned modules</td></tr><tr><td>6: real evaluation</td><td>CH-15 after CH-12/14, tentative Nov 3–6 at Tambunan National High School, Marinduque (previous Nov 8–13); CH-25 after CH-15/23</td><td>Approved sessions and actual evidence required; manuscript structure may be prepared, results may not be invented</td></tr><tr><td>7: defense package</td><td>CH-16 after CH-01/22/23/25 by Nov 16; CH-17 after CH-14/15/16 by Nov 20</td><td>Nov 17–20 reserved for critical fixes, offline rehearsal and backups</td></tr><tr><td>8: remaining cycle</td><td>CH-18 Nov 27; CH-19 Dec 18; CH-20 Feb 8, 2027; CH-21 Apr 9, 2027</td><td>Post-panel scope and school dates require confirmation; preserve carried targets</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="integration-and-acceptance-workflow">Integration and acceptance workflow<a class="header-anchor" href="#integration-and-acceptance-workflow" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><ol><li>Coordinator checks the current GitHub issue and dependencies, repository state and accepted contracts. Never infer implementation status from this plan or an open/closed issue alone.</li><li>Create an isolated branch/worktree where supported (codex/ prefix); preserve other working changes. Assign an exact file allowlist. If a shared checkout is required, separate paths strictly and reserve shared files to the integrator.</li><li>Agent implements only its packet, using synthetic data. Run relevant rule/contract checks and report device checks still pending. Commit Unity metadata with assets. Only the integrator changes Packages, ProjectSettings, bootstrap scenes, shared assemblies or the canonical schema unless explicitly delegated for one packet.</li><li>Handoff includes task ID, base/head commit, file list, requirements covered, test output, build/content/schema versions, remaining gaps and a demonstration recipe. Another worker&#39;s uncommitted edits must not be included.</li><li>Named reviewer examines correctness, educational meaning and regressions. Merge one packet at a time; update consumer fixtures if an approved contract changes. Agent self-tests alone do not establish acceptance.</li><li>Integrator builds the combined APK; Miguel executes assigned device cases, Christian reviews technical results. Confirm airplane-mode play, suspend/relaunch, valid saved progress and safe error behavior at each slice.</li><li>Update the existing issue with reviewed acceptance evidence when authorized. Keep published IDs and Trello mapping intact; update backlog publication metadata only after confirmed remote writes. This document task did not publish changes or move cards.</li></ol><p>No gameplay completion claim follows from generated code, a green editor test or a document heading. Evidence levels are planned, implemented, device-verified and evaluated. Record the actual level for each requirement.</p><h2 id="definition-of-ready-and-done">Definition of ready and done<a class="header-anchor" href="#definition-of-ready-and-done" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>Ready: prerequisite acceptance is linked; scope/contract versions are named; ownership paths do not collide; synthetic fixtures exist; unresolved decisions are either excluded from this packet or block it explicitly. A draft can explore a blocked question, but cannot be merged as the settled production decision.</p><p>Done: the exact requirement&#39;s acceptance passes, reviewer records disposition, integrated build is identified where applicable, documentation is consistent, and material limitations are explicit. CH-R01/09/11 require real-device evidence. CH-R12 additionally needs school-required manuscript and pilot evidence. No calculation based on task counts proves 60 percent completion.</p><h2 id="milestones-capacity-and-contingency">Milestones, capacity and contingency<a class="header-anchor" href="#milestones-capacity-and-contingency" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>The <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/kanban-plan-draft.html#october-8-revised-forecast-tentative-november-36-school-beta">revised October 8 forecast</a> keeps Oct 9–11 device/SQLite proof, Oct 18 first lesson slice/classifier/protocol and Oct 25 integrity/replicas/export/art; moves AI/agent to Oct 28, integrated candidate to Oct 30 and readiness QA to Oct 31–Nov 2. School beta is tentative Nov 3–6; Nov 7–13 is reserved for fixes, evidence validation and analysis, with no assumed repeat visit. Manuscript remains Nov 16, rehearsals Nov 17–20 and defense Nov 21. All dates except defense are conditional; original issue dates remain unchanged externally.</p><p>The <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/team-delegation.html#october-8-workload-balancing-proposal">balanced workload proposal</a> previously allocated 520 hours for the superseded scope; this has not been recosted (Sherwin 104, Christian 108, Sean 104, Luis 100, Miguel 104), including 12 contingency hours each. The previous 355–530-hour envelope is historical. Availability remains unknown; this budget is neither measured remaining effort nor a promise of capacity. Luis takes asset import, scene dressing, figures and visual QA; Miguel takes UI, routine device execution, synthetic fixtures and manuscript assembly. Preserve named specialist review. AI access supplies no measured productivity multiplier.</p><p>Merge reviewed increments Oct 18, 22, 25 and 28, with Christian owning shared-scene integration and Miguel running smoke/regression cases. The Oct 30 candidate gate must not be the first combined build. Each gate names build/content/schema versions and unresolved defects. No agent may certify a device check it did not run.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Trigger</th><th>Required response</th><th>Protected outcome</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>APK/SQLite spike misses Oct 11</td><td>Christian records failure, narrows binding/toolchain experiment, Sherwin reforecasts dependencies</td><td>Do not accumulate features on an unverified Android foundation</td></tr><tr><td>First lesson slice not accepted Oct 18</td><td>Reduce scene/asset quantity, pair on integration, obtain adviser decision if scope/date conflict persists</td><td>Retain required concepts; protect November QA and rehearsal</td></tr><tr><td>Site/instruments unresolved</td><td>Sherwin/Sean pursue named contact and actual approvals immediately; block learner sessions</td><td>Required pilot evidence cannot be replaced by synthetic results</td></tr><tr><td>Schema/API disagreement</td><td>Stop affected merges, resolve one version and rerun producer/consumer fixtures</td><td>Stable save and export interpretation</td></tr><tr><td>Oct 30 candidate or Nov 2 readiness failure</td><td>Freeze features; Sherwin/Sean resolve school booking and adviser evidence implications; Christian prioritizes core blockers</td><td>No unsafe learner session or assumed repeat visit; preserve manuscript/rehearsal time</td></tr><tr><td>Board-sync credential expiry Nov 4</td><td>Sherwin follows existing tooling operations guide privately</td><td>Management continuity; no learner runtime dependency</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="open-decision-register">Open decision register<a class="header-anchor" href="#open-decision-register" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Decision owner</th><th>Blocks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Actual Android models/toolchain (zero implementation reconfirmed)</td><td>Christian, with Sherwin confirmation</td><td>CH-03/04 validation and trustworthy forecast</td></tr><tr><td>Unknown sustainable hours: user requested balanced estimates</td><td>Sherwin records actuals against the proposed budget</td><td>Validated capacity commitments; drafting the estimate can proceed</td></tr><tr><td>Rubric criteria, 60-percent definition, exact ISO instrument</td><td>Sherwin/Sean obtain school guidance</td><td>CH-01/23 and defense claims</td></tr><tr><td>Named pilot contact, booking, permissions, custodian/destination</td><td>Sherwin/Sean with site</td><td>CH-24/12/15</td></tr><tr><td>Accepted content caps, quiz thresholds and teacher-code procedure</td><td>Sherwin with educational/technical review</td><td>CH-02 and affected content/persistence contracts</td></tr><tr><td>Individual availability against the balanced workload proposal</td><td>Each member; Sherwin consolidates</td><td>Validated dispatch commitments; agent work remains under human ownership</td></tr></tbody></table><p>User clarified on October 8 that “background schema” means the SQLite database schema. No cloud backend is implied.</p><h2 id="board-taxonomy-and-publication">Board taxonomy and publication<a class="header-anchor" href="#board-taxonomy-and-publication" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>Proposed optional labels: type:design/build/test/research, stream:foundation/gameplay/ai/ui-art/evidence, platform:android, decision:proposed/confirmed. Inspect existing remote labels before adopting; retain current labels until an intentional migration. Do not create duplicate CH issues. Issue/card status is the execution record; this plan and the generated packet snapshot do not compete with it.</p><p>The existing 26 CH issues were read on October 8 and all were open; this checks identity, not project-board status or implementation progress. Refresh live issue/board evidence before each dispatch. No new issue, comment, notification, task schedule or automation was created by this plan.</p><h2 id="parallel-documentation-and-media-delivery-before-game-implementation">Parallel documentation and media delivery before game implementation<a class="header-anchor" href="#parallel-documentation-and-media-delivery-before-game-implementation" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>For the requested complete GDD, use three workers plus the coordinator. Worker A owns the GDD draft only; worker B owns media files, captions and provenance manifest; worker C owns an independent validation report and reads all outputs without rewriting authors&#39; files. The coordinator owns baseline/design-brief/schedule reconciliation and integration. In this run, coordinator dispatch may assign a dedicated planning worker first, then reuse its slot for validation. Never exceed the available slots or give simultaneous ownership of one file.</p><p>Shared input contract: the user&#39;s template and attached references; current baseline/PRD/GDD rules; immutable mechanic IDs; first two attachments as definite visual style, third as gameplay/context only; device specs and rubric unchanged; approved bounded replacements and classifier-validation gate; full free-spatial construction deferred. Draft text and assets can proceed concurrently after that contract is recorded. Media generation receives the same character/world/UI direction and produces accompanying illustrations, not claimed game screenshots. Preserve original reference media with explicit provenance; generated media must be labelled.</p><p>Exit gate: coordinator incorporates actual asset paths and captions, then a validator other than the author checks every requested template section, image role, rule/flow cross-reference, scope boundary, schedule arithmetic and known unknown. Authors correct findings in their own paths; coordinator performs the final link and consistency review. Human acceptance remains with Sherwin, Christian for technical claims, Sean for learning/research wording, Luis for art and Miguel for UI.</p><p>Subsequent coding waves use exclusive rules/UI/records/content directories and the contract table above. A completed media/GDD packet permits contract review; it does not bypass CH-03/04 device/store proof, school permissions, or the original finish-to-start dependencies.</p></div>',1)])])}const m=t(r,[["render",o]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
